course: physics
lesson: 39
title: Day and night, and the Moon's faces
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The Sun seems to climb up in the east and sink in the west, but it is really the Earth spinning towards the east, carrying you round like a passenger on a smooth, silent roundabout.
explain: Half of the Earth always faces the Sun and has day, and the other half faces away and has night, so when you go to bed in Britain, children in Australia are having breakfast.
explain: We do not feel the spin because it is perfectly smooth; you only feel motion when it speeds up, slows down or jolts.
explain: The Moon makes no light of its own: it is always half lit by the Sun, and as it goes round the Earth we see different amounts of that lit half, from a thin crescent to a full Moon.
explain: The Moon spins exactly once for each trip round the Earth, so we always see the same face, and its far side is not dark at all: it gets just as much sunshine.
fact: The Earth spins towards the east, so the Sun seems to rise in the east and set in the west; careful watching proved it is the Earth that turns.
fact: Half the Earth always faces the Sun and has day while the other half has night.
fact: One spin of the Earth takes about 24 hours and makes one day and one night; long ago the Earth spun faster, and the Moon's pull slowly brakes it.
fact: At the equator the ground moves at about 1,600 kilometres an hour, but we feel nothing because the motion is smooth.
fact: The whole night sky seems to turn round one point because the Earth spins; the North Star sits almost above the North Pole and hardly moves, and sailors used it to find north.
fact: The Moon shines with reflected sunlight; it is always half lit, and we see different amounts of the lit half as it goes round the Earth.
fact: The Moon spins once for each orbit of the Earth, so we always see the same face; its far side was first photographed by a spacecraft in 1959.
fact: In an eclipse of the Sun the Moon passes exactly between the Sun and the Earth; the Sun is about 400 times wider than the Moon but also about 400 times further away, so they look the same size.
fact: An eclipse of the Sun must never be looked at without special eclipse glasses.
fact: In an eclipse of the Moon the Earth's shadow covers the Moon, and sunlight bent through our air turns it red, as if lit by all the Earth's sunsets at once.
fact: The Moon is often in the sky in daytime and is bright enough to see; the stars are there too, but the bright sky hides them.
fact: It is a myth that the far side of the Moon is always dark: "dark" once meant unknown, and at new Moon the far side is in full sunshine.
exercise: Why does the Sun seem to rise in the east? | The Earth spins towards the east | The Sun goes round the Earth from the east | The wind blows the Sun up from the east | The east is closer to the Sun
exercise: Why is it night in Australia when it is day in Britain? | The two places are on opposite sides of the spinning Earth | The Sun goes to Australia only at night | Australia is under a big cloud at that time | Australia is much further from the Sun
exercise: How much of the Earth faces the Sun at any moment? | About half | All of it | About a quarter | Only the equator
exercise: Why does a day last about 24 hours? | That is how long the Earth takes to spin once | That is how long the Sun takes to go round the Earth | That is how long the Moon takes to go round the Earth | Clocks were made to say so
exercise: Were days always 24 hours long? | No, long ago the Earth spun faster and days were shorter | Yes, they have never changed at all since the Earth began | No, long ago days were much longer | Yes, but only since clocks were invented
exercise: Why don't we feel the Earth spinning? | The spin is smooth, with no bumps or sudden changes | The Earth spins far too slowly for anyone to notice it | The air holds us still | We are too heavy to be moved
exercise: About how fast does the ground move at the equator as the Earth spins? | About 1,600 kilometres an hour | About 16 kilometres an hour | About 160,000 kilometres an hour | It does not move at all
exercise: Why does the North Star hardly move in the night sky? | It sits almost above the North Pole, round which the sky seems to turn | It is the closest star to the Earth | It is fixed to the sky by gravity | It moves too fast to see
exercise: How did sailors find north at night without a compass? | By finding the North Star | By watching which way the waves went | By following the brightest planet | By listening to the wind
exercise: Why does the Moon change shape through the month? | We see different amounts of its sunlit half as it goes round the Earth | The Earth's shadow covers part of it each night | It grows and shrinks like a balloon | Clouds on the Moon hide parts of it
exercise: Where does the Moon's light come from? | It reflects sunlight | It makes its own light | It glows from hot rocks inside | It reflects the lights of our cities
exercise: Why do we always see the same face of the Moon? | It spins once each time it goes round the Earth | It does not spin at all | The other side is too dark to see | The Earth blocks the view of the other side
exercise: When was the far side of the Moon first photographed? | In 1959, by a spacecraft | In 1609, through a telescope | In 1969, by the first astronauts | It has never been photographed
exercise: How can the small Moon cover the huge Sun in an eclipse? | The Sun is about 400 times wider but also about 400 times further away | The Moon swells up during an eclipse | The Sun shrinks for a few minutes | The Moon is closer to the Sun than the Earth is
exercise: Why is the Moon red during an eclipse of the Moon? | Sunlight bent through the Earth's air lights it red | The Moon gets very hot and glows | Dust storms blowing across the Moon turn it red for a while | The Sun turns red for a few hours
exercise: Why can't we see the stars in the daytime? | The bright sky hides them, though they are still there | The stars go out during the day | The stars hide behind the Sun | The stars move round to the other side of the Earth by day
test: my bro says the Sun goes round us, look it moves across the sky innit. right? | No, the Earth spins, so the Sun only seems to move | Yes, the Sun goes round us once every day, east to west | Yes, but only in summer | No, the Sun stays still and the sky moves
test: "The dark side of the Moon is always in darkness," says a band poster. Put it right. | The far side gets as much sunlight as the near side | It is right, that side never gets any sunlight at all | It is lit only by the Earth | The Moon has no far side
test: Granny on the phone from Sydney says "good morning" as you go to bed. How can that be? | Sydney is on the side of the Earth now turning to face the Sun | She is confused about the time | Australia has two suns | The Sun goes to Sydney first every day
test: You are on a plane flying very smoothly and your juice does not spill. How is this like the spinning Earth? | Smooth motion cannot be felt, only bumps and changes | The plane is not really moving, it only seems to be moving | Planes and the Earth both stand still | Juice is too heavy to move
test: tbh I saw the Moon at like 10 in the morning, is something broken lol? | No, the Moon is often up in the daytime | Yes, the Moon only ever comes out at night | Yes, that was just a pale round cloud, not the Moon | No, that was the Sun shining dimly through haze
test: In a cartoon, a crescent Moon has a star shining inside its dark part. What's wrong? | The dark part is still the Moon, so it would hide any star behind it | Nothing, stars are closer than the Moon | The crescent should be blue | The Moon has a hole in it
test: Leo wants to watch a solar eclipse with sunglasses. What should he do? | Use special eclipse glasses only; sunglasses are not safe | Sunglasses are fine if he squints and only looks for a minute | Look quickly, a few seconds is safe | Look through binoculars instead
test: What would happen to the Moon's face as seen from Earth if the Moon stopped spinning? | Over each month we would see all sides of it | We would still see only the very same one face | The Moon would go completely dark and disappear | The Moon would stop and fall down to the Earth
test: The Moon is full tonight and looks orange-red at one point during an eclipse. What is shining on it? | Sunlight bent through the Earth's air, like sunsets | Red lava glowing from old volcanoes on the Moon's surface | Reddish light reflected onto it from the planet Mars | Light from all the big cities on the night side of Earth
test: Why can the Sun and the Moon look the same size in the sky? | The Sun is much bigger but also much further away | They really are exactly the same size, just in different places | The Moon is bigger than the Sun | The air makes them look the same
test: A camera points at the North Star all night with the shutter open. What does the picture show? | Circles of starlight round the North Star | A single still dot for each and every star | The stars all falling slowly down to the ground | A black sky with nothing in it at all, all night
exam: Identify the cause of the apparent passage of the Sun across the sky from east to west. | The rotation of the Earth towards the east | The revolution of the Sun about the Earth | The motion of the clouds from east to west | The pull of the Moon upon the Sun
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the same hemisphere of the Moon is perpetually presented to the Earth. | The Moon rotates once upon its axis in each revolution about the Earth | The Moon does not rotate upon its axis at all | The opposite hemisphere is permanently unlit | The Earth obscures the opposite hemisphere
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the phases of the Moon? | Varying portions of its sunlit half are visible as it orbits the Earth | The Earth's shadow obscures varying portions of it each night | The Moon emits varying quantities of its own light | The Moon alters in actual size through the month
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the observation of a solar eclipse. | It must never be viewed save through special eclipse glasses | It may be viewed safely through ordinary sunglasses | It may be viewed directly provided the duration of viewing is brief | It may be viewed safely through binoculars
